{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:InformationTheory.BoltzmannConstant","@type":"LeanModule","name":"InformationTheory.BoltzmannConstant","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.QuantumObservableUniverse","lean:module:QOU.MathConstants","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.gasConstant_factored","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.gasConstant_factored","kind":"theorem","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/boltzmann-constant.lean#L101-L108","doc":"The gas constant factors as N_A · ℏ_q where N_A = ℏ_q² / Vol(B₃). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.gasConstant_classical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.gasConstant_classical","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/boltzmann-constant.lean#L94-L99","doc":"The gas constant vanishes at classicality. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.gasConstant_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.gasConstant_pos","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/boltzmann-constant.lean#L84-L92","doc":"The gas constant is positive when q > 1 and Borromean volume is positive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.gasConstant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.gasConstant","kind":"def","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/boltzmann-constant.lean#L77-L82","doc":"**Gas constant** (leading order).\nR_q ≈ ℏ_q³ / Vol(B₃) where Vol(B₃) is the Borromean volume.\nSince k_B = 1, R = N_A where N_A = ℏ_q² / Vol(B₃). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.thermalEnergy_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.thermalEnergy_pos","kind":"theorem","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/boltzmann-constant.lean#L67-L75","doc":"The thermal energy is positive when q > 1. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.ideal_gas_thermal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.ideal_gas_thermal","kind":"theorem","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/boltzmann-constant.lean#L58-L65","doc":"The quantum ideal gas law in terms of thermal energy:\nPV = N · thermalEnergy = N · ℏ_q at leading order.\nThis is the equation PV = N k_B T with k_B = 1. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.inverse_temperature_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.inverse_temperature_eq","kind":"theorem","line":49,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/boltzmann-constant.lean#L49-L56","doc":"Inverse temperature is the reciprocal of the thermal energy. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.beta_times_thermalEnergy","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.beta_times_thermalEnergy","kind":"theorem","line":35,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/boltzmann-constant.lean#L35-L47","doc":"The canonical ensemble identity: β · T = 1.\nWith β = q/(q-1) and T = (q-1)/q = ℏ_q, the product is unity.\nThis is the content of the Boltzmann identification: inverse\ntemperature is the reciprocal of the thermal energy. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.thermalEnergy","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.thermalEnergy","kind":"def","line":26,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/boltzmann-constant.lean#L26-L33","doc":"**Thermal energy** at a point p ∈ M.\nThe product k_B · T_phys in classical physics corresponds to ℏ_q\nin QOU. Since k_B = 1 in natural units, the thermal energy IS\nthe quantum temperature: thermalEnergy = T_q = ℏ_q.\nLabel: def:boltzmann-constant ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:def:boltzmann-constant","label":"def:boltzmann-constant","md":"quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/boltzmann-constant.md"}],"signatureChunk":0}]}